A correct Matlab solution defines the state vector, initial data, parameters, right-hand side, and calls an ODE solver such as `ode45`.

```matlab
N = 17000000;
alpha1 = ...;     % infection rate variant 1
alpha2 = ...;     % infection rate variant 2
beta1  = ...;     % recovery/removal rate variant 1
beta2  = ...;     % recovery/removal rate variant 2

S0 = N - 340 - 1;
I10 = 340;
I20 = 1;
y0 = [S0; I10; I20];

tspan = [0 100];  % choose final time as required

rhs = @(t,y) [-(alpha1*y(2) + alpha2*y(3))*y(1)/N;
              alpha1*y(2)*y(1)/N - beta1*y(2);
              alpha2*y(3)*y(1)/N - beta2*y(3)];

[t,y] = ode45(rhs,tspan,y0);

S  = y(:,1);
I1 = y(:,2);
I2 = y(:,3);

plot(t,S,t,I1,t,I2)
legend('S','I_1','I_2')
xlabel('time')
ylabel('number of individuals')
```

Equivalently, the right-hand side may be written as a separate function:

```matlab
function dydt = variants(t,y,alpha1,alpha2,beta1,beta2,N)
    S = y(1);
    I1 = y(2);
    I2 = y(3);

    dydt = zeros(3,1);
    dydt(1) = -(alpha1*I1 + alpha2*I2)*S/N;
    dydt(2) = alpha1*I1*S/N - beta1*I1;
    dydt(3) = alpha2*I2*S/N - beta2*I2;
end
```

called by

```matlab
[t,y] = ode45(@(t,y) variants(t,y,alpha1,alpha2,beta1,beta2,N),tspan,y0);
```
